{
  "reconstructed_theorem_text": "For every complete finite-dimensional real inner-product space E equipped with its compatible Borel measurable structure and canonical Haar/Lebesgue measure λ = volume, every real-valued potential U : E → ℝ of class C², and every pair α, β of nonnegative real constants with α > 0, suppose that for all x, v ∈ E,\n    α ‖v‖² ≤ D²U(x)[v,v] ≤ β ‖v‖².\nHere α and β are the real coercions of their nonnegative-real inputs, and D²U(x)[v,v] is the iterated Fréchet derivative evaluated twice on v.\nDefine μ = λ.tilted(-U). In this definition the exponential density exp(-U) is normalized against λ when it is integrable; otherwise the tilt is the zero measure. Thus, on the integrable branch, μ(dx) = Z⁻¹ exp(-U(x)) λ(dx), where Z = ∫ exp(-U(x)) λ(dx). This displayed measure and its probability status are not input assumptions.\nThe conclusions, jointly, are: μ is a probability measure; the Hilbert gradient ∇U is Bochner integrable with respect to μ; and\n    ∫_E ∇U(x) μ(dx) = 0_E.\nThe gradient is the real Hilbert Riesz representative of DU, so DU(x)[v] = ⟨∇U(x),v⟩. The integral is an E-valued Bochner integral and its first integrability is a proved output. There is no assumption α ≤ β, β > 0, normalization of exp(-U), or positive dimension. When dim E = 0, E contains only 0_E, all directional inequalities have zero on both sides, and the gradient and its mean are zero; α > β is allowed in this case.",
  "objects": "Input E is a real finite-dimensional complete Hilbert space with compatible Borel measurable structure. Input U is E → ℝ. Input α, β belong to ℝ≥0, with real coercions used in Hessian inequalities. λ is the given canonical volume measure. μ is defined from λ and U by normalized exponential tilt. ∇U(x) is an E-vector representing the real Fréchet derivative. The zero in the conclusion is 0_E, not a scalar zero.",
  "domains": "x and v range over all E. The potential is real-valued. Integrals of the gradient range over E and are E-valued Bochner integrals with respect to μ. E may have real dimension zero. The Borel structure is compatible with the norm topology.",
  "quantifiers": "Universal inputs E, its stated structures, U, α and β; hypotheses α > 0, U of class C² and the conjunctive two-sided Hessian bound for every x and v. A let-definition then fixes μ. Three conclusions are conjoined. There is no existential selector, extra measure input or dimension restriction.",
  "assumptions": "Complete finite-dimensional real normed inner-product space; measurable space and Borel compatibility; U is C²; α and β are nonnegative-real inputs; α is strictly positive; α ‖v‖² ≤ D²U(x)[v,v] and D²U(x)[v,v] ≤ β ‖v‖² for every x and v. There is no explicit α ≤ β premise: that follows from a nonzero direction when E has positive dimension, but is not available or required in dimension zero. No μ-probability or gradient-integrability hypothesis is supplied.",
  "conclusion": "The defined tilted measure μ is a probability measure, ∇U is Bochner integrable under μ, and its actual Bochner integral is zero. These are joint produced outputs. The probability conclusion rules out a zero-mass tilt under the hypotheses. The theorem states no quantitative bound on an integral, covariance or moment beyond the stated integrability and zero mean.",
  "scopes": "α and β are fixed for the whole potential and all x and v. Hessian bounds are global in x and all directions v. μ is fixed by U and volume and does not take α or β as definition arguments. The expectation conclusion has no free spatial parameter. The theorem includes the zero-dimensional case with vacuous directional curvature constraints.",
  "constant_dependencies": "No additional existential constant occurs. α > 0 supplies the input lower curvature and β ≥ 0 the input upper curvature. The normalizer Z, when expressing the successful tilt as a density, depends on U and the specified E/volume, not on arbitrary externally supplied normalization. No α ≤ β or positive β requirement is imposed. No quantitative dependence on d, α or β is asserted.",
